-gate-bigotry-eugenics-and-the-law-that-kept-two-generations-of-jews-italians-and-oth"],"publicationYear":2019,"thumbnailUrl":"https://covers.openlibrary.org/b/id/8806705-L.jpg","readerProfile":{"readerLevel":"crossover","narrativeScore":0.221,"accessibilityScore":0.058,"scholarlyScore":0,"traits":[{"id":"narrative","score":0.48,"confidence":"medium","evidence":["catalog text contains \"story of\"","catalog text contains \"tells the story\""]},{"id":"argument_driven","score":0.18,"confidence":"low","evidence":["catalog text contains \"argument\""]}],"method":"deterministic_catalog_heuristics_v1"},"experimentalSemanticProfile":{"centralFigures":[],"centralPlaces":[{"name":"United States","confidence":0.88}],"argument":{"present":true,"statement":"Eugenicist claims about ethnic hierarchy supplied intellectual justification for restrictive U.S. immigration laws and influenced racial policies in Nazi Germany.","confidence":0.96},"academicOrientation":{"score":32,"confidence":0.88},"profileConfidence":0.86,"lowerConfidenceCandidates":{"centralFigures":[{"name":"Calvin Coolidge","confidence":0.46}],"centralPlaces":[{"name":"America","confidence":0.5}]},"model":"gpt-5.6-luna","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"e925263ed6b554eca3efbad9bf15a03d98666a77573ea97f23353082b27a7bb3","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","validationWarnings":[]},"summary":"Eugenicist arguments ranking the presumed genetic virtue of various ethnic groups helped keep hundreds of thousands of Jews, Italians, and other unwanted groups out of the United States for more than forty years. By 1921 Vice President Calvin Coolidge declared that 'biological laws' had proven the inferiority of southern and eastern Europeans; the restrictive law that remained U.S. policy until 1965 was enacted three years later. Okrent connects the work of the American eugenicists to Nazi racial policies and shows how their beliefs found fertile soil in the minds of citizens and leaders both here and abroad. -- adapted from jacket \"From Pulitzer Prize finalist Daniel Okrent, the definitive and timely account of a forgotten dark chapter of American history. The Guarded Gate tells the story of the scientists who provided the intellectual justification for the harshest immigration law in American history and the men who turned their 'science' into politics. Brandished by the upper-class Bostonians and New Yorkers--many of them progressives--who led the anti-immigration movement, eugenicist arguments ranking the presumed genetic virtue of various ethnic groups helped keep hundreds of thousands of Jews, Italians, and other unwanted groups out of the United States for more than forty years.","subjectCategories":[{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Eugenics","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-guarded-gate-bigotry-eugenics-and-the-law-that-kept-two-generations-of-jews-italians-and-oth"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Sterilization (birth control)","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-guarded-gate-bigotry-eugenics-and-the-law-that-kept-two-generations-of-jews-italians-and-oth"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Discrimination in medical care","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-guarded-gate-bigotry-eugenics-and-the-law-that-kept-two-generations-of-jews-italians-and-oth"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Human reproduction, law and legislation","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-guarded-gate-bigotry-eugenics-and-the-law-that-kept-two-generations-of-jews-italians-and-oth"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Emigration and immigration law","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-guarded-gate-bigotry-eugenics-and-the-law-that-kept-two-generations-of-jews-italians-and-oth"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"New York Times reviewed","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-guarded-gate-bi